What is the effect of pH on the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles in a solution of known ionic strength?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The pH of a solution has a significant effect on the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles in a solution of known ionic strength. The electrokinetic potential, also known as the zeta potential, is the potential at the slipping plane, which is the boundary between the bulk liquid and the charged layer at the particle surface. The pH effect is primarily due to the protonation and deprotonation of functional groups on the particle surface and the resulting change in the surface charge.

The effect of pH on zeta potential can be understood as follows:

1. Surface charge: Colloidal particles often have a surface charge due to the ionization of functional groups on their surface. The pH of the solution affects the ionization state of these functional groups, which in turn affects the surface charge of the particles.

