","a) **Serial System**:  
   - A serial series is where the circuit is closed only if all components are in the correct configuration.  
   - The reliability of a series circuit is the product of the reliability of each component.  
   - If \( N \) components are connected in series, and each component has a reliability function \( R_1(t), R_2(t), \ldots, R_N(t) \), the reliability of the system is:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = \begin{cases}
     1 \quad \text{for } t = 0 \\
     R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot \ldots \cdot R_N(t) \quad \text{for } t < t_0 \\
     0 \quad \text{for } t \geq t_0
     \end{cases}
     \]  
     where \( t_0 \) is the time at which all components fail.  

b) **Parallel System**:  
   - A parallel series is where the circuit is closed if one component is in the correct configuration.  
   -The reliability of a parallel circuit is the complement of the complement of the reliability of each component.  
   - If \( N \) components are connected in parallel, and each component has a reliability function \( R_1(t), R_2(t), \ldots, R_N(t) \), the reliability of the system is:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = 1 - \left(1 - R_1(t)\right) \cdot \left(1 - R_2(t)\right) \cdot \left( 1 - R_3(t) \right) \cdot \left\{ \begin{cases}
     \ldots \cdot \left( 1 - R_N(t) \right)
     \end{cases} \right)
     \]  
     This can also be written as:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = \begin{cases}
     1 \quad \text{for } t = 0 \\
