The maximum height \( R \) is the maximum distance the particle moves from the ground. At maximum height, the velocity of the particle is zero. Using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2as \]  
where:  
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (final velocity at maximum height)  
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity)  
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity, negative because it is downward)  
- \( s = R \) (distance, which is the maximum height)  

Substituting the values:  
\[ 0 = 20^2 + 2(-9.8)R \]  
\[ 0 = 400 - 19.6R \]  
\[ 19.6R = 400 \]  
\[ R = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.41 \, \text{m} \]  

Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

