ΔH° = Σ ΔHf°(products) - Σ ΔHf°(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy change, and ΔHf° is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The products are Ca(OH)2(s) and the reactants are CaO(s) and H2O(l). Plugging the given values into the equation:

ΔH° = [1 × (-986.2 kJ/mol)] - [1 × (-635.1 kJ/mol) + 1 × (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 288.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 288.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -986.2 + 635.1 + 288.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -192.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is -192.3 kJ/mol.
