1. The slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( a \), which is \( f'(a) \).
2. The tangent line passes through the point \( (a, f(a)) \).
3. The equation of a line is in the form \( y = mx + b \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( b \) is the y-intercept.
4. Substituting \( m = f'(a) \) and the point \( (a, f(a)) \) into the equation \( y = mx + b \):
   \[
   f(a) = f'(a)(a) + b \implies b = f(a) - af'(a)
   \]
5. Substituting \( b \) back into the equation \( y = mx + b \):
   \[
   y = f'(a)x + f(a) - af'(a)
   \]
6. Rearranging the equation to match the standard form:
   \[
   y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
   \]

This is the equation of the tangent line to \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \). 

