- \(m_e = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\) is the mass of the electron,
- \(c = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\) is the speed of light,
- \(c_0 = 3 \000000 \, \text{m/s}\) is the speed of light in vacuum.

The maximum possible change in wavelength occurs when the electron is scattered with a scattering angle of 180° (i.e., the photon is scattered away). This is the Compton effect, where the electron's momentum is conserved. The change in momentum of the photon is equal to the momentum of the electron. The maximum possible momentum of the electron is:
\[
