This reaction is a two-step process:
1. **Anode (oxidation):**
   \[ \text{Pb} + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Pb}^{2+} + 2\text{H}^+ \]

2. **Cathode (reduction):**
   \[ \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{PbO} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} + text{H}_2 \]

3. **Net reaction:**
   \[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}^+ + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{Pb(OH)}_2 + \text{H}_2 \]

Thus, the overall reaction is the reduction of lead (Pb) and the oxidation of lead dioxide (PbO₂), with the formation of lead hydroxide (Pb(OH)) and hydrogen gas (H₂).
