","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is given by the formula:
\[
\Delta \lambda / \lambda = \frac{2E_e}{m_e c^2}
\]
where:
- \(\Delta \lambda = \Delta p\) is the change in momentum of the photon,
- \(\lambda\) is the initial wavelength of the photon,
- \(E_e = \frac{m_e c^2}{2m_e} = \frac{c^2}{2v}\) is the kinetic energy of the electron,
- \(m_e = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\) is the mass of the electron,
- \(c = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\) is the speed of light,
- \(c_0 = 3 \000000 \, \text{m/s}\) is the speed of light in vacuum.

The maximum possible change in wavelength occurs when the electron is scattered with a scattering angle of 180° (i.e., the photon is scattered away). This is the Compton effect, where the electron's momentum is conserved. The change in momentum of the photon is equal to the momentum of the electron. The maximum possible momentum of the electron is:
\[
p_e = \frac{m_e c}{2}
\]
The momentum of the photon is:
\[
p_{\text{photon}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda} = \frac{hc}{\lambda_0}
\]
The change in momentum of the photon is:
\[
\Delta p = p_{\text{final}} - p_{\text{initial}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda_{\text{final}}} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_{\text{initial}}}
\]
For a 180° scattering, the final momentum of the photon is \(\frac{hc}{\lambda_{\text{final}}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda_0} + m_e c\). Thus, the change in momentum is:
\[
\Delta p = m_e c
\]
The fractional change in wavelength is:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} = \frac{\Delta p}{p_0} = \frac{m_e c}{h c / \lambda_0} = \frac{m_e \lambda_0}{h}
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} = \frac{(9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}) \times (2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m})}{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s}} = 2.74 \times 10^{-10}
\]
Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} \times 100 = 2.74 \times 10^{-10} \times 100 = 2.74 \times 10^{-8}
\]

The maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{2.74 \times 10^{-8}}\).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
